---
title: "The browser client"
description: "The browser half, which does almost nothing: mounting blocks, applying ops, the idempotent enhancer registry, and why the preview never writes back."
---

The server does the rendering; the browser does almost nothing. The client
(`web-client/`) is plain vanilla JavaScript, no framework and no build step, that
mounts the server's HTML and then applies the [block diff](block-model.tmd) one op
at a time. There is no virtual DOM and no reconciliation pass of its own: the
*server* decided what changed, and the client's whole job is to splice those few
blocks into a live page without disturbing the rest. Math is rendered server-side,
so there is nothing to re-run on the client; code highlighting is too. The result
is a thin layer whose hardest problem is *not* breaking the things the diff worked
to preserve.

The client is four files, each shipped as one `<script>` with no bundling:

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `web-client/client.js` | Mounts the render, applies ops, reverse sync, click-to-source, the dev menu. Type-checked (`// @ts-check`). |
| `web-client/search.js` | The Cmd-K command palette. |
| `web-client/toc-spy.js` | The table-of-contents scrollspy. |
| `crates/core/assets/js/code-enhance/` | The enhancer registry plus the built-in enhancers (copy buttons, reader menu, lightbox, …). |

`client.js` is the only one that talks to the websocket. The other three are
self-contained features that ride along beside it, and the static `build` ships
the same `search.js` / `toc-spy.js` / `code-enhance/` so a built page behaves
like the preview.

## Mounting and applying ops

`client.js` mounts into a single element, `#tali-root`; without it the script is a
no-op. On connect the server sends a `full_render` (and the page body was *already*
server-rendered into `#tali-root` so content paints before the socket opens), and on
each save it sends only the [diff ops](block-model.tmd). The full message contract
is documented in [the block model](block-model.tmd); here is what the client does
with each one.

The first paint is the subtle case. Because the body is server-rendered, the very
first `full_render` after connect is **identical** to what is already on screen, so
the client *skips* the destructive part of mounting it (a `window.TALIESIN_SSR` flag,
consumed once): no `resetJs()` teardown of `{js}` cells and no `root.innerHTML`
replacement, so there is no flash. It does *not* skip the `afterChange()` pass that
runs after every render, so the deck is still initialized (the first `syncDeck`
calls `TaliesinDeck.initialize`) and the code enhancers still run over the SSR'd DOM. A
reconnect, by contrast, re-mounts normally, and a dropped socket retries on a
one-second loop (`onclose` → `setTimeout(connect, 1000)`).

For the incremental ops, every block is found by its `data-block-id`
(`elById(id)`, a `[data-block-id="…"]` query with `CSS.escape`):

- **`update`** finds the target and `replaceWith`s a fresh element built from the
  HTML, then pulses it (`tali-flash`) so the change is visible.
- **`insert`** builds the element and places it after `after_id` (or `prepend`s it
  when `after_id` is null). Because block ids are unique, it first drops any element
  already carrying the incoming id; the server emits all removes before any inserts,
  so this is normally a no-op, and only matters if a reorder's `remove` + `insert` of
  the same id ever arrived out of order.
- **`remove`** drops the element by id.
- **`set_meta`** patches position attributes on the *existing* element and **does
  not re-render it**: it always sets `data-sourcepos`, and it sets `data-source-file`
  when the op carries one or *removes* it otherwise. This is the path a block takes
  when an edit above it shifted its line numbers without changing its content: its
  HTML is byte-identical (same content-hash id), so re-rendering would needlessly
  rebuild it and drop its live DOM state, but its source mapping is now stale.
  Patching the two attributes in place keeps both the live state *and* exact
  click-to-source.

The other server messages are handled too: `diagnostics` repaints the issue panel,
`style` hot-swaps the theme `<style id="tali-theme">` in place (no reload, so scroll
and the current slide survive a theme edit), `error` raises a full-screen overlay
over the last good content, and `reload` (sent after a kernel restart or a config
change) does a plain `location.reload()`.

The traffic is almost entirely server → client; the client sends only two messages
back over the socket:

```js
{ type: "click_block", block_id?, source_file, sourcepos }  // on a Ctrl-click
{ type: "restart_kernel" }                                  // from the dev menu
```

`click_block` is the websocket half of forward click-to-source (sent alongside the
`vscode://`/`postMessage` jump); `restart_kernel` is the Restart-kernel button. Both
go out only when the socket is `OPEN`.

### The dev control bar

The preview's only chrome is one collapsed corner widget, `#tali-controls`: a `</>`
toggle carrying the live-status dot, expanding to a panel of preview-only tools. The
panel shows the connection status, the word/reading-time count, the Ctrl-click hint,
a Restart-kernel button, and — only when the page chrome ships no theme toggle of
its own, which is the case for a document previewed outside any project — a theme
toggle. It also aggregates three issue sources into one list, each
row jumping to the offending source line: server `diagnostics` (broken include,
missing kernel), per-cell runtime errors (Python `.tali-error`, `{js}`
`.tali-js-error`), and an in-browser a11y audit run after every render (missing alt
text, heading-level skips, links/buttons with no accessible name, a missing
`<html lang>`, and body text below WCAG AA contrast). The total issue count shows as
an amber badge on the collapsed toggle, so a problem is visible without expanding
the panel. In deck mode (no control bar) the status survives as a standalone pill.

### Scroll and live-state preservation

The diff guarantees an unchanged block is never touched. The client's job is to not
spoil that for the blocks that *are* touched, and the work is concentrated in two
small functions.

`keepScroll(fn)` wraps every mutation: it reads `window.scrollY`, runs the
mutation, then restores the scroll position with `behavior: "instant"` so the
restore is never animated even on a page with smooth scrolling. Without it, an
`update` to a block above the viewport would shift everything below and bump the
reader's place.

`afterChange()` runs after each mount and re-attaches everything that lives
*outside* the block model: it re-syncs the deck, rebuilds the live TOC, re-inits the
scrollspy against the fresh nav, recomputes the word count, re-runs the code
enhancers (`window.taliEnhanceCode(root)`), and re-scans for cell errors and a11y
issues. `set_meta` deliberately **skips** `afterChange()`, because nothing was
rendered.

::: {.callout-note}
## Why `{js}` cells get an explicit teardown

When an `update`, `insert` (of a stale duplicate), or `remove` detaches a block,
the client first calls `teardownJs(el)`. A `{js}` cell registers an `invalidation`
promise; `teardownJs` resolves it, so the author's
`invalidation.then(() => renderer.dispose())`-style cleanup runs and the cell is
spliced out of the `{js}` runtime's registry. Without this, editing a Three.js cell
(whose content-hash id changes, so the block is replaced wholesale) would leak a
WebGL context and an animation-frame loop on every save. A wholesale re-mount calls
`resetJs()` first, tearing down *every* cell so the runtime rebuilds clean. Both are
no-ops when `tali-js.js` is not loaded (a deck or a page with no `{js}` cells).
:::

## Reverse sync: `highlightAtLine`

Click-to-source runs in both directions off the same `data-sourcepos` attribute.
The reverse direction, editor cursor to preview block, is `highlightAtLine(file,
line)`. It walks every `[data-sourcepos]` element under `#tali-root` and, filtering
to blocks whose `data-source-file` matches the cursor's file, parses each position
with one regex:

```js
/^(\d+):\d+-(\d+):\d+$/
```

A block whose `data-sourcepos` does not match this shape (the synthesized title
block and the references section carry an empty string) is skipped, because it has
no source line. Among the matches the function picks the **smallest block whose
`[start, end]` range contains the line**, breaking ties by the narrowest span so a
nested block beats its container. If no block *contains* the line (the cursor is on
a blank line between blocks), it falls back to the **nearest block starting before
it**.

The chosen block gets a `tali-hl` outline (any previous one is cleared first), and
then the view follows the cursor:

- in a normal page, the block is scrolled into view only if it is off-screen
  (`getBoundingClientRect` outside the viewport), with `block: "center"`;
- in a **deck**, scrolling makes no sense, so the client finds the
  `.tali-slides > section` containing the block and jumps the deck to it with
  `window.TaliesinDeck.slide(i)`.

This is the same smallest-containing-block model the [deck engine](deck-engine.tmd)
and the source map were built around, run backwards.

## The `postMessage` bridge

The client speaks two `postMessage` messages so an editor host (an `<iframe>`) can
own the preview and add cursor sync without forking any code. The in-repo
[VS Code companion](extending.tmd) is that host, but the bridge is generic: any
parent frame can use it.

| Direction | Message | Payload | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| client → parent | `tali-goto` | `source_file`, `sourcepos` (plus `block_id` on a `data-block-id` click) | Forward click-to-source: reveal this source line in the editor. |
| parent → client | `tali-cursor` | `file`, `line` | The editor cursor moved; highlight/scroll to the covering block. |

`block_id` rides along only on the `data-block-id` click path; a located-diagnostic
click and a `data-tali-src` click post just `{ source_file, sourcepos }`. The host
needs only the file and line, so the field is informational.

The client decides which transport to use from whether it is framed
(`window.parent !== window`). When it is framed, Ctrl-clicking a block (or clicking a
located diagnostic) posts a `tali-goto` up to the parent rather than navigating;
the host turns that into an editor `revealRange`. When it is *not* framed, the same
gesture opens a `vscode://file/<path>:<line>:<col>` deep link directly. Inbound,
`window.addEventListener("message", …)` listens for `tali-cursor` and calls
`highlightAtLine(m.file, m.line)`.

::: {.callout-important}
## The preview never writes back

The bridge is deliberately one-way as a *write* path: click-to-source and
`tali-cursor` both only **navigate** and **highlight**. The `.tmd` file is the single
editing surface and the preview is a read-only view, so nothing the client posts can
mutate the source. There is no `tali-edit` message, by design.
:::

Forward click-to-source is a modifier *gesture*, not a mode: a plain click always
browses normally, and holding Alt (Option on Mac) turns the cursor into a pointer
over every source-mapped block (`html.tali-srcnav`) and outlines the exact block a click
would resolve to. Both the affordance and the click resolve the target through the
same `locatable()` helper (nearest `[data-tali-src]` or `[data-block-id]` ancestor),
so the highlight and the jump can never drift apart.

## The enhancer registry

`code-enhance/` opens with the one public client-side extension point:
`window.taliEnhancers`. An **enhancer** is a function `fn(root)` that decorates
freshly-mounted DOM, and an extension's JS opts in with one call:

```js
window.taliEnhancers.register(function (root) {
  root.querySelectorAll(".my-widget:not([data-enhanced])").forEach(function (el) {
    el.dataset.enhanced = "1"; // idempotency guard, see below
    // …decorate el…
  });
});
```

A registered enhancer runs:

- after **every** (re)mount in the live preview, via `afterChange()` →
  `window.taliEnhanceCode(root)`;
- once on `DOMContentLoaded` in the static build;
- once immediately when it registers *after* the page is already mounted (the
  registry tracks a `mounted` flag, so a deferred extension script loaded from
  `_site.yml` `head:` still gets to run on the existing DOM).

::: {.callout-warning}
## Enhancers must be idempotent

Because an enhancer re-runs on every change, it must be safe to run twice on the
same element. The convention is a data-attribute guard: select with
`:not([data-enhanced])` and set `data-enhanced` as the first thing you do. The
built-in copy-button enhancer does exactly this (`if (pre.dataset.enhanced) return;`
then `pre.dataset.enhanced = "1"`), and every built-in follows the same pattern.
:::

The built-ins register through this exact API, so a third-party enhancer is
indistinguishable from core's. The notable ones:

- **Copy buttons** (`taliCopyButtons`) on every `pre > code`, using a clipboard
  helper that falls back to a hidden-textarea `execCommand` in insecure contexts
  (`file://`, a plain-HTTP `--host` LAN preview).
- **`{js}` cells** (`tali-js.js`): finds each `<script type="application/tali-js">`,
  runs the author's code with a small scope, and mounts the result into its sibling
  target `<div>`. It is idempotent through a `data-tali-ran` guard and drives the
  reactive graph; the [interactive cells chapter](../guide/using/interactive.tmd)
  covers the runtime.
- **Mermaid** (`mermaid.js`): renders `{mermaid}` cells, lazy-loading the Mermaid
  library from the vendored copy — inlined into a static build, served same-origin in
  preview, so neither path touches the network. `TALIESIN_MERMAID_URL` overrides the
  loader URL in either mode.
- The **reader menu** and its features (theme / text-size / spacing prefs, focus
  mode, reading position + resume), the **image lightbox**, **anchor permalinks**, the
  **link-preview card**, the **keyboard shortcuts** layer, the **skip link**, and
  the listing **category filter**.

`window.taliEnhanceCode(root)` is the single entry point every caller (live client,
static build) uses to run the whole list.

## Cmd-K search (`search.js`)

`search.js` is a self-contained command palette, opened with Cmd-K (Ctrl-K off Mac),
by clicking any `[data-tali-search]` control, or programmatically via
`window.taliOpenSearch()`. It injects its own themed overlay CSS and is *not* part of
the type-checked `client.js` bundle.

Its index has two sources:

- **A single document** builds the index from the live DOM on open, walking every
  anchored heading (`h1[id]`…`h4[id]`) and the body text that follows it up to the
  next heading. Building from the DOM means the index reflects live edits with no
  server round-trip.
- **A site or book** reads `window.TALIESIN_SEARCH_INDEX`, the cross-page index the
  build emits (each entry is a page title plus its anchored headings, carrying the
  page's url so a result can navigate across chapters). To keep that index from
  bloating every page, the site links to it via `window.TALIESIN_SEARCH_URL` and
  `search.js` **lazy-loads `search-index.js` on first open** (a "Loading…" row shows
  meanwhile), caching it onto `TALIESIN_SEARCH_INDEX`.

**With no query, a book shows its whole outline**, not just its chapters: every page
followed by its sections, in reading order, each section indented by its depth *within
that chapter* (never its absolute heading level, which varies with whether the chapter
emits a title block and where it roots). That makes the section records already in the
index reachable by browsing rather than only by typing.

Matching is a multi-term score with field boosts: a title hit outranks a body hit,
with bonuses for an all-title match, a title-leading match, and an exact contiguous
phrase. Terms of four or more characters also match by edit-distance-1 typo
(`within1`, a bounded no-matrix Damerau-Levenshtein, so an adjacent transposition is
one edit and not two). An item that matches **some** terms is kept and ranked below
every full match, with a struck-through `Missing: <term>` line saying what was not
honoured. Command-palette **actions keep the old hard AND**: they are scored by the
same function and pinned above content, so relaxing them would surface "Toggle light /
dark theme" above the prose the reader asked for.

Results on a site or book **group by page**, each group led by its chapter row and
capped at a few sections with a "+N more in this chapter" disclosure, so one dense
chapter cannot monopolise the visible rows. The cap lifts when every hit is on the
same page (there is nothing to balance against). A chapter row is selectable only when
that page's own record matched; otherwise it is an inert label, so <kbd>Enter</kbd>
always lands on a real result. The cursor starts on the **best-scoring** row rather
than the first, because a chapter row sits at the top of its group for structure, not
because it scored best. Selecting a result on another page navigates there anchored to
the heading; on the current page (or in a single doc) it scrolls in place.

## TOC scrollspy (`toc-spy.js`)

`toc-spy.js` highlights the table-of-contents entry whose section currently sits
just under the sticky navbar. It is the canonical scrollspy shared by two callers so
the behaviour matches exactly: the static build inlines it and lets it auto-init on
load, and the live preview rebuilds `#TOC` on every edit and then calls
`window.taliInitTocSpy()` to re-collect against the fresh links.

On each scroll (throttled to one `requestAnimationFrame`) it finds the last heading
above the activation line, toggles `tali-toc-active`, expands only the active entry's
branch of a long nested TOC, and keeps the active link visible inside the TOC's own
scroll area. It also tracks **read state**: every entry strictly before the current
section is marked read (a forward-only high-water index, so scrolling back never
un-marks). That read set is the reader's own, stored in `localStorage` keyed by path
and anchored to each heading's stable `data-block-id`, so it survives reflow and
never touches the source: the same read-only, content-hash-anchored model the
resume feature uses.

